# `=` in a `join ... on` condition: DS0110.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId = $right.customerId
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..81
  PIPELINE@0..80
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    JOIN_OP@20..80
      IDENT@20..24 "join"
      WHITESPACE@24..25 " "
      LITERAL@25..40
        STRING@25..40 "'customers.csv'"
      WHITESPACE@40..41 " "
      IDENT@41..43 "on"
      WHITESPACE@43..44 " "
      BIN_EXPR@44..80
        FIELD_EXPR@44..60
          NAME_REF@44..49
            DOLLAR_LEFT@44..49 "$left"
          DOT@49..50 "."
          NAME_REF@50..60
            IDENT@50..60 "customerId"
        WHITESPACE@60..61 " "
        EQ@61..62 "="
        WHITESPACE@62..63 " "
        FIELD_EXPR@63..80
          NAME_REF@63..69
            DOLLAR_RIGHT@63..69 "$right"
          DOT@69..70 "."
          NAME_REF@70..80
            IDENT@70..80 "customerId"
  WHITESPACE@80..81 "\n"

error[DS0110]: `=` is assignment, not comparison
  --> query:1:62
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId = $right.customerId
  |                                                              ^ assignment is not allowed here
  |
  = help: use `==` to compare values: `$left.customerId == $right.customerId`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId == $right.customerId
which parses without errors
